  "worked_solution": "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+8 & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ -2{x}^{2}+8 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273827/assets/336a1503663239debacda06ca200e47151493ffbc94c3b797afb274c3355db97.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=-3{x}^{2}+8.$\n\nWe can now set up the integration with our $x$ values (Top-Bottom):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[(-3{x}^{2}+8)-(-{x}^{2})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(-2{x}^{2}+8)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(8-2{x}^{2})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the integrand is an even function. Therefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(8-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2((8\\cdot 2-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {2}^{3})-0) \\\\  & =2((16-\\frac{16}{3})-(0)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
